Why this design is strong:
            
            The naive implementation could, for every candidate cell:            
                        scan entire previous board
                        → check column
                        → check both diagonals
            
            That makes each safety check expensive.            
            Our design turns the question:    "Is (row,col) safe?"
            
            into three constant-time lookups:            
                                                usedColumns[col]
                                                usedDiag1[diag1]
                                                usedDiag2[diag2]
            
            So the constraint-checking itself is:   O(1) 
            
            That is an important optimization—not because the overall problem becomes polynomial, 
            but because we reduce the cost inside every node of the search tree.

================================================================================================================================================
Complexity:
              N-Queens has an exponential search space.
              
              A commonly used upper-bound characterization is approximately:              
                                                                              O(N!)
              
              because:               
                      row 0 has up to N choices,
                      row 1 has at most N-1,
                      row 2 at most N-2,
                      and so on.
              
              With pruning, the actual number of explored states is much smaller 
              for many inputs, but the problem remains exponential.

================================================================================================================================================
For each complete solution, building the board costs O(N²) if we materialize N strings of length N.

          So an interview-safe discussion is:
                                              Search: approximately O(N!)
                                              Constraint check: O(1)
                                              Output construction: O(N²) per solution
                                              Auxiliary state: O(N²) for board + O(N) tracking + O(N) recursion

The exact tight complexity discussion is less important than demonstrating that you 
understand where the exponential cost comes from and that constraint checks are constant-time.
