One more L6-level improvement:

              For even stronger implementation, the three boolean arrays can be replaced with bitmasks.              
              Then:              
                      columns
                      diag1
                      diag2
              
              can each be represented as an integer/long bitset, and we can determine all available positions using bit operations.
              
              That reduces the per-row search to something like:              
                            available = fullMask & ~(columns | diag1 | diag2)              
              and lets us extract available columns efficiently.
              
              For a Java interview, I would first present the boolean-array solution because it 
                is clearer and easier to prove correct, then discuss bitmasks as an 
                  optimization when the interviewer asks how to improve it.
              
              That is the kind of progression I want you to practice:              
                          Correct → clear → efficient → optimized when justified.
